WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.311
Accountability Scorecard.

00:00:02.261 --> 00:00:06.625
Every Canadian oversight institution graded on three simple questions.

00:00:06.625 --> 00:00:08.443
Can it investigate?

00:00:08.443 --> 00:00:10.159
Can it prosecute?

00:00:10.159 --> 00:00:12.852
What is its actual enforcement record?

00:00:12.852 --> 00:00:17.250
The answer across every institution is the same: they can investigate.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.295
They cannot prosecute.

00:00:19.295 --> 00:00:21.920
And their enforcement record is zero.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.431
This is why the prosecution framework exists — because you cannot report state crime to the state.

00:00:27.431 --> 00:00:29.068
The scorecard.

00:00:29.068 --> 00:00:34.477
Ethics Commissioner: can investigate, cannot prosecute, zero criminal consequences.

00:00:34.477 --> 00:00:40.329
RCMP: can investigate and prosecute, but zero prosecutions for political crimes.

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:45.443
MPCC: can investigate military police, cannot compel compliance.

00:00:45.443 --> 00:00:49.931
Procurement Ombudsman: can investigate fraud, cannot lay charges.

00:00:49.931 --> 00:00:54.090
NSIRA: can review intelligence, cannot direct operations.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.397
CEIPP: can monitor elections, cannot protect individual ridings.

00:00:58.397 --> 00:01:04.806
Information Commissioner: can investigate ATIP delays, cannot compel disclosure of cabinet confidences.

00:01:04.806 --> 00:01:09.897
PSIC: can receive whistleblower disclosures, rarely finds wrongdoing.

00:01:09.897 --> 00:01:12.147
Every body can investigate.

00:01:12.147 --> 00:01:14.352
None effectively enforce.
